# {{ .name }}

.NET loader integration. An ASP.NET service subscribes to the `{{ .topic }}`
topic of the Dapr pub/sub: the sidecar POSTs each delivered message to the
subscription endpoint, which runs it through the Intropy loader pipeline and
writes the result as `{orderId}.json` through the destination binding. The
loader is a long-running service (a Deployment in production).

## How a message flows

```
sidecar POSTs CloudEvent from '{{ .topic }}'
   │
   ▼
deserialize ──▶ validate ──▶ transform
   │
   ▼
idempotency check
   │
   ▼
write {orderId}.json to the destination
   │
   ▼
ack SUCCESS ──▶ message consumed
```

Off the happy path: duplicates and routed business incidents also ack SUCCESS
(the message is consumed, never written); a technical failure acks RETRY and
the sidecar redelivers until the destination recovers.

Settings live in `src/appsettings.json`, overridable by
`appsettings.{Environment}.json` and environment variables; deployment
overlays the file via ConfigMap and `IConfiguration` is in DI. The
platform-service app ids (`PlatformServices:*`) come from the scaffold
parameters `idempotencyAppId`/`businessIncidentsAppId`.

## Build and test

```bash
task build
task test
```

The loader does not run standalone. It runs via its system host, which
provides the pub/sub, destination binding, and platform services, and
carries the sample data used to publish a message to the topic. `task test`
covers the component-level loop: pipeline unit tests plus integration tests
proving the whole DI graph and the delivery path behave without a sidecar
(`task test:unit` / `task test:integration` run the suites separately).
`task coverage` runs the integration suite with branch coverage (Cobertura
report under the integration project's `TestResults/`).

## Testing strategy

The integration suite runs against `Intropy.Framework.Testing` fakes — no
Dapr sidecar, no Testcontainers, no live platform services. The one principle
throughout: **boot the host exactly as production composition does, fake
only the edges, assert on fake state and the delivery ack.**

### Layers

| Layer | Project / files | What it pins |
|---|---|---|
| Step unit tests | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Unit/Process/*Tests.cs` | Each pipeline step's own contract: `Deserializer` (valid payload, business id and CloudEvent subject/time stamped into the context), `Validator` (one test per business rule), `Transformer` (field fidelity), `Sender` (writes through the adapter; the one NSubstitute seam). |
| Delivery integration | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/DeliveryIntegrationTests.cs` | The full pipeline (deserialize → validate → idempotency → transform → send) driven end-to-end: `WebApplicationFactory<Program>` boots the real host with the externals faked, and `DaprDelivery.DeliverAsync` POSTs CloudEvents to the subscription route exactly as a sidecar would. Pins the ack contract: loaded, duplicate, and incident-routed messages ack SUCCESS; a dead destination acks RETRY and leaves no trace.{{- if .empty }} In this empty-bodies scaffold two tests are not included: business-failure incident routing (which input fails validation is the Validator's to define), and duplicate-ack (the duplicate simulation relies on the idempotency commit the implemented pipeline records). Add them as the corresponding implementation lands.{{- end }} |
| Boot / composition | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/BootSmokeTests.cs`, `CompositionTests.cs` | The host builds without a sidecar and announces its subscription via `GET /dapr/subscribe`; the DI graph from `Composition` resolves the pipeline and the keyed destination adapter. |

### The edges and their fakes

- **Destination files** — `InMemoryFileAdapter` at the keyed
  `IFileAdapter` seam, with fault injection: `WriteException` simulates a
  dead destination (the RETRY path).
- **Platform services** — `FakeIdempotencyServiceClient` and
  `FakeBusinessIncidentServiceClient`. Tests assert on recorded state
  (`Committed`, `Incidents.Single().Data.Description`) directly.
- **Delivery** — `DaprDelivery.DeliverAsync` POSTs the structured-mode
  CloudEvents envelope as `application/cloudevents+json` and parses the
  `{"status": "..."}` ack; unknown or malformed statuses map to RETRY,
  matching the sidecar's fail-safe redelivery.

### Behaviors pinned

- **Never retry a poison message.** Invalid input routes a business incident
  and acks SUCCESS; a business failure does **not** commit idempotency (a
  corrected redelivery must not cancel as seen-before).
- **A duplicate is a traceable no-op.** The idempotency check cancels the
  delivery; the message is acked but never written or committed again.
- **A dead destination redelivers.** A write failure acks RETRY with nothing
  written, committed, or incident-routed — the sidecar retries until the
  destination recovers.

### What is deliberately not tested

No Testcontainers or real Dapr sidecar: the delivery tests already prove the
envelope handling and ack mapping at near-zero cost. Revisit only if the real
sidecar's structured-mode or metadata behavior is ever suspected of
diverging.

## Shared contracts

The canonical contract types (`{{ .contract }}`, `OrderLine`) live in the sibling
`../{{ .contracts }}` project, referenced from `src/{{ .name }}.csproj`. It was
scaffolded together with this project (or already existed — components in the
same parent directory share it); the publishing extractor serializes exactly
those records. Only the destination load record (`Out`) stays local in
`src/Model/`.

## Container image

```bash
docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .
```

Multi-stage build on the .NET 10 chiseled runtime. The shared contracts sibling
enters the build as the `contracts` named context (it lies outside this build
context). The image contains only the app — the Dapr sidecar and components
come from the deployment environment.

## Pipeline

Per delivered message (`src/Endpoints/LoaderEndpoints.cs` rebuilds the
envelope into a CloudEvent and drives this):

```text
// Loader pipeline transitions:
//
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 1. Deserializer             │
//         └──────────────┬──────────────┘
//                        │ CloudEvent.Data → shared {{ .contract }}; stamps context
//                        ▼
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 2. Validator                ├──── failure ───► Business Incident Service
//         └──────────────┬──────────────┘
//                        │ required fields + business rules pass
//                        ▼
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 3. Idempotency check        ├──── duplicate ─► [[Cancelled]]
//         └──────────────┬──────────────┘
//                        │ Subject / Time is new
//                        ▼
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 4. Transformer              │
//         └──────────────┬──────────────┘
//                        │ shared {{ .contract }} → Out
//                        ▼
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 5. Sender                   │
//         └─────────────────────────────┘
//                        writes {orderId}.json through destination binding
//
// Success, duplicates, and business incidents return [[AckSuccess]].
// Technical failures from any step return [[AckRetry]] for sidecar redelivery.
```
{{- if .empty }}

1. `Deserializer` parses `CloudEvent.Data` into the shared `{{ .contract }}` contract.
2. `Validator` enforces business rules.
3. Idempotency check — cancels duplicates.
4. `Transformer` maps `{{ .contract }}` → `Out`.
5. `Sender` writes `Out` through the destination binding.

Every step body throws `NotImplementedException` — this project was scaffolded
with empty bodies for a migration agent to fill in. The idempotency and
business-incident wiring is already in place; the idempotency step keys off
CloudEvent Subject/Time from the context, so backfill those from stable
business fields in your `Deserializer` (see `AGENTS.md`).
{{- else }}

1. `Deserializer` parses `CloudEvent.Data` into the shared `{{ .contract }}` contract
   (from `../{{ .contracts }}` — the same record the extractor publishes), stamps
   `order_id` into the pipeline context, and stamps the `subject`/`time`
   context keys from the order's `OrderId`/`OrderDate` — for
   extractor-published events these equal the envelope values, and a
   `dapr publish` envelope carries no subject and a wall-clock time.
2. `Validator` enforces required fields and basic business rules; failures are
   routed to the Business Incident Service.
3. Idempotency check — asks the Idempotency Service whether this Subject /
   Time combination was already processed; duplicates cancel the run.
4. `Transformer` maps the canonical order to the load record `Out`
   (`LoadedAt` = now, `Status` = `"Loaded"`).
5. `Sender` serializes `Out` with default System.Text.Json (PascalCase) and
   writes `{orderId}.json` through the destination binding.

Success, an idempotent duplicate, and a routed business incident all
acknowledge the message (`status: SUCCESS` in the response body); a technical
failure returns `status: RETRY` and the sidecar redelivers.
{{- end }}
